### 3. The `'e'` Exit File & `Xn` Bypass
Right now `'e'` acts as an explicit **allow-list** of 2-char sector exits on every border crossing (`Vn/Vs/Ve/Vw`) and on `Vt` (unless `XnVt` is used).

The pain point is boilerplate: in an open grid world (or `desktop.gfx`), having to populate `e` on every single sector just to allow normal N/S/E/W scrolling is tedious. Two clean ways to simplify `'e'` while keeping 1984 compatibility:
* **Presence-based or flag-based `'e'`:** Keep `'e'` strictly enforced when `w/<sector>/e` exists and is non-empty (or when `/a/gfxStrictExits` / a `W` flag is on). If a sector has no `'e'` file—or in open-world mode—allow normal boundary scrolling (`calculateTargetSector`) automatically, and still use `'e'` where you want walls/locked borders or special `Vt` destinations.
* **`XnVt` bypass:** This is already great for sysop/power-up teleports. Just note there is currently a small bug in `UNIVAC`’s `Vt` handler: it checks `if (!ifnot)`, **but never resets `ifnot = false` afterward**, so `XnVt` leaves `ifnot` turned on for the next command on the tape!

